WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.917
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Hi.

00:00:00.917 --> 00:00:03.660
I'm Priyanka Vergadia, and this
is "AI Simplified," where we

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.910
learn to make our data useful.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:09.030
In this video, we are
predicting the future.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:12.630
That sounds like I'm an
astrologer, but not really.

00:00:12.630 --> 00:00:15.660
We are going to predict a
different kind of future

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.700
influenced by data.

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:21.330
Yeah, you're right, we're
covering forecasting models--

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:25.290
what they are and how to
create them in Vertex Forecast.

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:27.240
But before we dive
into it, what are

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:29.430
some of the applications
of forecasting?

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:31.290
Say you're a
retailer and you need

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:34.530
to predict product
demand or sales,

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.530
or you're a call center manager
and you're predicting the call

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.290
volume to hire more
representatives,

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.810
you're a hotel chain
predicting hotel occupancy

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:46.590
for next season, or you're a
hospital and forecasting bed

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:47.730
occupancy.

00:00:47.730 --> 00:00:49.050
You get the idea.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.660
Forecasting data sets come
in many shapes and sizes.

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:54.930
There are univariate
data sets, where

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:59.050
we observe a single variable
over a period of time.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:03.690
For example, you can think about
weather data or airlines data.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.000
Even though it's
just one variable,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.340
sometimes this is all
that you need to forecast

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:10.320
your data into the future.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.880
You can see clear patterns,
the seasonal peaks, and also

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.470
the trends in that data.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:20.070
More often, we are faced
with multivariate data sets,

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:23.400
where you have historical
observations of the variables

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.790
you're forecasting and
other related time series.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:31.710
These are often referred to
as covariates, and covariates,

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:36.240
because the value varies
with the target variable.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.900
These related time series can
be very helpful to forecast

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.400
your target variable.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.860
And maybe, for example, you're
plotting rain versus umbrella

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.850
sales versus Uber rides.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:51.120
Now, we all know how taxis can
be hard to get when it suddenly

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:51.870
starts to rain.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:54.180
So as an owner of
a taxi company,

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.480
I can use the weather forecast
to anticipate the higher

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.040
demand in my cars and
bring the cars online.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.190
Be aware that not all
covariates are useful,

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:08.340
even if they correlate strongly
with your target variable.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.289
Some common examples
of covariates

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:14.790
used in retail forecasting are
holidays, third-party forecasts

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:17.040
such as weather,
and you can have

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:21.090
planned promotions, foot traffic
to the stores, and so on.

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:25.860
Up next, we are going to look
at forecasting algorithms.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:29.310
Now, here are the algorithms
we can use for forecasting.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.880
The most popular methods
today are statistical methods,

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.850
which are also called
as classical methods.

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:38.710
There are many types
of statistical models,

00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:42.960
including ARIMA models, that are
being used for most popular use

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:43.830
cases.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:47.310
Now, Google offers an
advanced ARIMA plus model

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:50.880
with BQML, which is BigQuery ML.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:54.180
The other class is
machine learning models,

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:58.710
like various tree-based
methods and neural networks.

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:00.570
Now, these deep
learning models have

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:04.170
been gaining a lot of popularity
for forecasting applications

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.682
over the past few years.

00:03:05.682 --> 00:03:07.140
Now, obviously,
there's still a lot

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:09.720
of debate on when to
apply which methods,

00:03:09.720 --> 00:03:12.210
but it's becoming
increasingly clear

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:14.460
that neural networks
are here to stay

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:16.620
for forecasting applications.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.750
These methods
perform fairly well

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.330
across a broad range of
forecasting problems,

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:24.750
and particularly well when
dealing with forecasting

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:26.670
newer item demands.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.370
And these are
called cold starts,

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:34.350
and forecasting cold starts on
items with limited or varying

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.180
historical data.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:38.550
Now, deep learning
models cross-learn

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:41.430
across very large groups
of related time series

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:44.130
with varying lengths
and missing data.

00:03:44.130 --> 00:03:46.200
The good news is
that you don't really

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.020
need to know exactly
which architecture to use

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:52.230
because Vertex Forecast
does that for you.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.180
Now, we can build
forecasting models

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:58.110
in Vertex using advanced auto
ML algorithms for neural network

00:03:58.110 --> 00:03:59.670
architecture search.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:03.000
Now, we automated the
preprocessing of your time

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.960
series data, and, instead
of fumbling into data types

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.610
and transformations,
you can just

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:12.030
load your data into the
data set in Vertex AI,

00:04:12.030 --> 00:04:14.460
and auto ML would
automatically apply

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:18.269
those common transformations
and even engineer features

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:21.450
for you required for modeling.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.480
Now, most importantly,
we could evaluate

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.140
hundreds of models in parallel
to find the right architecture

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:29.880
for your particular data set.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.210
And this requires a lot of
computational resources.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.750
But because we can run all
these trials in parallel,

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.930
we're still able to
find the best model

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.210
and tune it to your
particular data

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.790
set in the fraction
of time it typically

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:46.950
takes for a traditional method.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:50.220
And of course, we can
assemble the best model

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.500
and together to
ensure the model is

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:56.320
stable across a wide range
of predicted outputs.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:57.850
Now, how does all this work?

00:04:57.850 --> 00:05:00.340
Let's jump into the
console and see.

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:04.990
From our Vertex AI dashboard, we
create a new tabular data set.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:08.290
We can see there is an
option for forecasting.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:10.870
You may be curious if you
can apply a regression

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:13.090
model to your time series data.

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:15.100
Now, the difference
between them is

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:17.710
that regression and
classification, the target's

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:20.980
predictive value depends only
on the values of the feature

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:22.720
columns in the same row.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:25.750
Now, in forecasting, the
target's predictive value

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:28.000
also depends on
the context values

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.400
of the target and the features.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.980
Now, we will see more on
this further in the demo.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:35.320
In regression and
classification problems,

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:37.580
the output is one value.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:40.630
But as you can imagine,
in forecasting problems,

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:44.180
the output is a
sequence of values.

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:47.950
Now let's select Forecasting
here and proceed.

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:52.210
We are using the Iowa Liquor
Sales public dataset available

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:53.950
in BigQuery.

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:57.340
Now, it consists of wholesale
liquor purchases in the United

00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:00.520
States state of Iowa
since 2012, and we

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:02.710
can preview this data here.

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:05.470
For the purpose of this
demo, I have already

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.410
done some basic data
preprocessing to group

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:10.870
the purchases by
day, and we will

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:15.130
use a CSV extract from
the BigQuery table.

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:18.130
Now, back in our data
set, let's import the data

00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:20.320
from our Cloud Storage bucket.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:23.740
You can also upload the data
directly from your computer

00:06:23.740 --> 00:06:26.290
or BigQuery table here.

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:28.420
Once the data is
imported, we can

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:30.580
configure the model features.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:32.920
The time series
identify column is

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:36.440
to specify what time
series a row belongs to.

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:39.220
Now, every unique
combination of key values

00:06:39.220 --> 00:06:41.530
defines a different time series.

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:46.090
For example, here in ID is
our time series identifier.

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:48.620
Now, if we need to
forecast on multiple time

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:52.330
series, such as product and
store levels, both of those

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:54.460
could be used as keys.

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:58.090
Now, time column is the
identifier, such as date

00:06:58.090 --> 00:06:59.770
or days, et cetera.

00:06:59.770 --> 00:07:03.340
Y is our sum of
purchases that day,

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:07.040
and holiday is a Boolean
to indicate a holiday.

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:08.950
Then we can train the model.

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:10.720
Select the target column.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:12.790
This is the value
we are predicting.

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:16.640
So in our case, it is
sales, or the Y column.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:20.530
We have already set our time
series identifier and the time

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:24.730
column, so next we need to
set the date of frequency.

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:28.390
I'm selecting hourly here and
setting the forecasting horizon

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:29.500
to 7.

00:07:29.500 --> 00:07:31.810
This specifies the
number of periods

00:07:31.810 --> 00:07:35.720
that the model can
predict into the future.

00:07:35.720 --> 00:07:39.590
Now, historical context window
I'm setting to 30 hours here.

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:44.020
This means that the model will
use data from previous 30 hours

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:45.730
to make a prediction.

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:48.730
There are trade-offs between
shorter and longer windows,

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:52.450
and generally selecting a
value between 1 to 5 times

00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:55.820
the forecasting
horizon is recommended.

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:57.610
Now let's check
the box to export

00:07:57.610 --> 00:07:59.980
test data set to BigQuery.

00:07:59.980 --> 00:08:02.350
We can leave it blank,
and it will automatically

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:07.480
create a data set and table in
our project within BigQuery.

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:10.990
Now, in the training options,
we can specify more details

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:13.450
about how we would like
to train the model.

00:08:13.450 --> 00:08:16.660
Set these columns to
available for a new forecast

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:19.180
because we know this
information already.

00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:22.030
Change the optimization
objective to MAE,

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:26.830
or Mean Average Error, which
is more resilient to outliers.

00:08:26.830 --> 00:08:29.980
And because we are working
with daily purchase data that

00:08:29.980 --> 00:08:33.880
can have wild fluctuations,
MAE is an appropriate metric

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:35.260
to use here.

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:38.470
Before we initiate the
training, set a budget.

00:08:38.470 --> 00:08:43.010
In this case, one node hour is
sufficient to train the model.

00:08:43.010 --> 00:08:45.010
Now, once the model
is trained, we

00:08:45.010 --> 00:08:47.920
can evaluate it and
go to BigQuery console

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:50.875
to view the predictions
on test data.

00:08:50.875 --> 00:08:53.200
Our new data set
is automatically

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:57.040
created where we find the
evaluated data items table

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:58.810
to review the predictions.

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:01.810
This table has a couple
of new columns here--

00:09:01.810 --> 00:09:04.150
the Predicted on
Date column, which

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:08.980
is the date that the predictions
were made, and Predicted Y

00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:12.260
column is the
predicted sales value.

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:15.040
Now, finally, we would
want to use your model

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:16.850
to make predictions.

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:21.310
We have an input file in the
CSV format on Cloud Storage,

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:24.100
and it contains MT
values for the dates

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:27.280
to be predicted along
with the historical data.

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:30.820
After it is complete, we can
click on the batch prediction

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:35.440
job to view the details,
including the export location.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.780
We sent our predictions
to BigQuery,

00:09:37.780 --> 00:09:42.610
which we can see here, and the
job creates two different files

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:43.960
or tables.

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:46.390
One contains any
rows with errors,

00:09:46.390 --> 00:09:48.520
and the other
contains predictions.

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:51.730
Lastly, now that we have
our batch predictions ready,

00:09:51.730 --> 00:09:54.820
we can evaluate our
forecasts in Data Studio,

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:58.250
which is Google's free
data visualization tool.

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:00.760
The blue line shows
my historical values,

00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:03.520
and the red lines
are my forecast.

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.260
Now, it looks like
the model picked up

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:08.080
on quite a bit of
signal in the data.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:11.590
So in this video, we learned
about forecasting models,

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:15.620
what they are, and how to
create them in Vertex Forecast.

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:18.220
Give this a try yourself,
and send me your questions

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:20.370
in the comments below.

